   200	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   201	    """
   202	    Assign scattered grains to the nearest reachable opening and pour them into frame interiors up to capacity.
   203	
   204	    Input structure:
   205	    - The background contains several one-cell-thick hollow rectangular frames of different colors.
   206	    - Most frames have exactly one missing border cell as an opening; some frames are fully closed.
   207	    - A single extra color forms scattered grain pixels outside the frames, and frame borders act as walls.
   208	
   209	    Rule:
   210	    - For each grain, compare 4-connected path distances through passable non-wall cells to every reachable opening; assign the grain to the uniquely nearest opening's frame.
   211	    - Each open frame fills its interior from the cell just inside its opening, ordered by interior path distance from that entry cell, breaking ties by row then column.
   212	    - If a frame receives more grains than interior capacity, only the closest assigned grains move; the rest stay in place, and every moved grain is erased from its source cell.
   213	
   214	    Key insight:
   215	    - Ownership depends on shortest paths around walls rather than straight-line proximity, and capacity truncation makes the grain ranking matter twice: once for frame assignment and again for deciding which sources disappear.
   216	    """
